<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Navigation Acts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1651">1651</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Navigation Acts were a series of English laws from the mid-seventeenth century that restricted colonial trade to English ships and English ports. Aimed in large part at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> commercial dominance, they were a cornerstone of English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a>. They helped build the shipping, trade and fiscal base on which the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> grew.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Dutch Republic — whose East India Company (VOC) long outperformed its English counterpart — and by the gradual development of the fiscal-military state that would underpin British global power: the <b>Navigation Acts</b> (from 1651), the Bank of England (1694), the funded national debt and a professional Royal Navy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Overseas commerce grew rapidly under the <b>Navigation Acts</b>, which reserved colonial trade for British ships: sugar from Jamaica and Barbados, tobacco from Virginia and Maryland and textiles from India were re-exported across Europe, and Glasgow prospered on tobacco after the union.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Industrial Britain abandoned protectionism: Richard Cobden and John Bright of the Manchester School campaigned for lower tariffs, and Britain repealed the Corn Laws in 1846 and the <b>Navigation Acts</b> in 1849, in line with David Ricardo’s case for free trade.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In Britain mercantilism mainly took the form of control of trade: tariffs on imports, bounties for exports, bans on exporting some raw materials, and <b>Navigation Acts</b> that kept foreign merchants out.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>The British revenue schooner HMS Gaspee, which enforced the <b>Navigation Acts</b>, ran aground off Warwick, Rhode Island, and that night men led by Abraham Whipple and John Brown boarded and burned her.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-9">9 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The regime passed the <b>Navigation Act</b> of 1651, fought the First Anglo-Dutch War and allowed some religious toleration while repressing Levellers and royalists.</blockquote><a href="/state/commonwealth-of-england">Commonwealth of England</a></li></ul></section>
